The matching goes on its own thread so bsd_init is not behind it

Run 546 and run 548 both stopped between S1 and S2.  Those two marks are
written immediately before and immediately after startCandidate(inst), so
one candidate's start() never came back, and everything after it -- bsd_init,
the root volume, launchd -- waits on the same thread.  That is the whole of
the silence after S1: not a hang in the kernel, a call that has not returned.

The matching does not need the boot thread.  It is started here instead, on
a thread of its own, and the boot thread goes on to bsd_init whatever a
personality does.  If a start() still does not return, the matching thread
says so and the kernel reaches the userland anyway -- which is the thing
every round since 540 has been waiting for.

Two marks name the path: "MT thread" means the matching is off this thread,
"MT inline" means kernel_thread_start refused and the old call is still in
place.  "MT run" and "MT done" bracket the matching itself, so the panel
says whether it returned.

The thread is created here rather than inside StartIOKitMatching() because
this is where the boot order is written down, and because the marks have to
go straight to the panel: nio_dc_puts() is the writer this file already uses
for the heartbeat and the beacon, and it does not go through the console
ring that stops.

--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -481,6 +481,34 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/* nio: the matching runs on its own thread.
+ *
+ * Run 546 and run 548 both stopped between the S1 mark and the S2 mark, and
+ * those two are written immediately before and immediately after
+ * startCandidate(inst), so one candidate's start() never came back.  Every
+ * phase that follows -- bsd_init, the root volume, launchd -- waits behind
+ * that call on the same thread, and that is the whole of the silence after
+ * S1.
+ *
+ * The matching does not have to hold the boot thread.  Started here, it runs
+ * on a thread of its own, and the boot thread goes on to bsd_init whatever
+ * any personality does.  If a start() still never returns, the panel says so
+ * and the kernel still reaches the userland.
+ *
+ * The marks say which path was taken: "MT thread" means the matching is off
+ * this thread, "MT inline" means the thread could not be created and the old
+ * call is still in place.  "MT done" later means the matching returned. */
+static void
+nio_matching_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
+{
+	(void) arg;
+	(void) wr;
+
+	nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT run");
+	PE_lockdown_iokit();
+	nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT done");
+}
+
 __startup_func
 void
 kernel_startup_initialize_upto(startup_subsystem_id_t upto)
@@ -901,7 +929,17 @@
 
 #ifdef  IOKIT
 	kernel_bootstrap_log("PE_lockdown_iokit");
-	PE_lockdown_iokit();
+	{
+		thread_t nio_mt = THREAD_NULL;
+
+		if (kernel_thread_start(nio_matching_thread, NULL, &nio_mt) == KERN_SUCCESS) {
+			thread_deallocate(nio_mt);
+			nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT thread");
+		} else {
+			nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT inline");
+			PE_lockdown_iokit();
+		}
+	}
 	printf("\nR4 iokit lockdown");
 #endif
 	/*
